Title: Point the npm package at this project's releases
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

src/electron/npm/ is the source of the `electron` npm package: index.js, cli.js,
install.js and a package.json that upstream publishes with name, version and the
rest injected at release time (there is no `npm publish` in the Electron repo
any more; that moved to external tooling). build.sh --npm assembles npm_package/
from these files, so customising the package means patching them here rather
than hand-maintaining a copy.

  npm/install.js    downloadArtifact() gains mirrorOptions. @electron/get
                    otherwise resolves
                      https://github.com/electron/electron/releases/download/
                    and a consumer would get upstream's binaries from a package
                    claiming to be ours. The URL it builds is
                      ${mirror}v${version}/electron-v${version}-${platform}-${arch}.zip
                    which is exactly the layout package() writes into dist/, so
                    uploading dist/v<version>/*.zip as release assets on the
                    matching tag is all it takes to serve it. mirrorVar() in
                    @electron/get reads env and npm config *before* the value
                    passed here, so ELECTRON_MIRROR and
                    npm_config_electron_mirror still override it.

  npm/package.json  the static half of what upstream injects at publish time:
                    name, description, repository, license, author, keywords.
                    Deliberately not version -- install.js reads that to build
                    the download URL, so it has to track ELECTRON_VERSION, and
                    --npm writes it when it assembles the package. Keeping the
                    name as "electron" is what lets this drop into a
                    package.json as a replacement: require('electron'), the
                    electron bin, and anything resolving the electron package
                    all keep working.

  npm/install.js    extractFile() uses extract-zip rather than upstream's
                    @electron-internal/extract-zip, and package.json swaps the
                    dependency to match. Upstream's is a Rust/napi addon, and
                    its bundled prebuild imports ProcessPrng from
                    bcryptprimitives.dll (Windows 10+) and WaitOnAddress from
                    api-ms-win-core-synch-l1-2-0.dll (Windows 8+), so it cannot
                    load on the systems this whole project exists for: `npm i`
                    on Windows 7 dies in its binding.js with "Cannot find
                    native binding", which is napi's generic message and not
                    the npm optional-dependency bug it points at -- the
                    prebuilds ship inside the package, so there is no optional
                    dependency to miss. extract-zip is the extractor Electron
                    used through v42 and is pure JavaScript down through yauzl,
                    so it has no OS floor of its own. It is slower, which costs
                    nothing on a one-time install.

  npm/package.json  a postinstall script, `node install.js`, so the binary is
  npm/install.js    downloaded by `npm install` and not on first use. That is
                    how the package worked for every Electron through v41;
                    upstream then dropped the script and left the download to
                    index.js, which fetches it the first time something
                    requires the package. For a small fork aimed at old
                    Windows that is the wrong default: after `npm install` the
                    package should be there to look at, copy to a machine with
                    no network, or run, like nearly every other package, and a
                    download that fails should fail the install that asked for
                    it and not some later, unrelated command.

                    index.js keeps its download-on-first-use, unchanged, as
                    the fallback for installs that run no scripts at all
                    (--ignore-scripts, or a package manager that blocks
                    dependency scripts by default); isInstalled() makes the
                    second attempt a no-op when the first one worked.

                    ELECTRON_SKIP_BINARY_DOWNLOAD comes back with it, since it
                    is the documented way to stop the old postinstall
                    fetching. It applies to the postinstall run alone --
                    install.js checks npm_lifecycle_event -- so setting it
                    defers the download to first use, and never leaves
                    `npx install-electron` or index.js unable to fetch.

Checksum verification is left on. upstream's install.js reads ./checksums.json,
and --npm generates it from the SHASUMS256.txt that package() already writes,
so there is no reason to disable it.

diff --git a/electron/npm/install.js b/electron/npm/install.js
--- a/electron/npm/install.js
+++ b/electron/npm/install.js
@@ -9,12 +9,32 @@
 
 const { version } = require('./package');
 
+// Where the release zips live. @electron/get builds
+// `${mirror}v${version}/electron-v${version}-${platform}-${arch}.zip`, which is
+// the layout build.sh's package() writes into dist/ and that these releases are
+// uploaded with. Env and npm config are read ahead of this value (see
+// mirrorVar() in @electron/get), so a consumer can redirect it with
+// ELECTRON_MIRROR or npm_config_electron_mirror without editing anything.
+const LEGACY_MIRROR = 'https://github.com/Alex313031/electron-legacy/releases/download/';
+
 const platformPath = getPlatformPath();
 
 if (isInstalled()) {
   process.exit(0);
 }
 
+// This runs as the package's postinstall script, so the binary arrives with
+// `npm install` -- as it did for every Electron through v41. v42 moved the
+// download to first use. ELECTRON_SKIP_BINARY_DOWNLOAD is the switch those
+// versions had for an install that should not fetch anything (a CI job that
+// only lints, a machine with no route to the release host), and it means the
+// same here. It is honoured for the postinstall run only: reached any other
+// way -- `npx install-electron`, or index.js finding no binary on first use --
+// this script has been asked for the binary and gets it.
+if (process.env.npm_lifecycle_event === 'postinstall' && process.env.ELECTRON_SKIP_BINARY_DOWNLOAD) {
+  process.exit(0);
+}
+
 const platform = process.env.ELECTRON_INSTALL_PLATFORM || process.env.npm_config_platform || process.platform;
 let arch = process.env.ELECTRON_INSTALL_ARCH || process.env.npm_config_arch || process.arch;
 
@@ -40,6 +60,7 @@
 downloadArtifact({
   version,
   artifactName: 'electron',
+  mirrorOptions: { mirror: LEGACY_MIRROR },
   force: process.env.force_no_cache === 'true',
   cacheRoot: process.env.electron_config_cache,
   checksums:
@@ -75,11 +96,21 @@
 
 // unzips and makes path.txt point at the correct executable
 function extractFile(zipPath) {
-  // Loaded here, not at the top of the file: the extractor has a native
-  // binding, and an OS policy can block its load (see electron/electron#52481).
-  // A lazy require keeps `isInstalled()` working in that case, so an
-  // already-installed dist does not need the extractor at all.
-  const { extract } = require('@electron-internal/extract-zip');
+  // extract-zip, not upstream's @electron-internal/extract-zip, which is the
+  // extractor Electron used through v42. Upstream's is a Rust/napi addon whose
+  // prebuilt index.win32-x64-msvc.node imports ProcessPrng from
+  // bcryptprimitives.dll (Windows 10+) and WaitOnAddress from
+  // api-ms-win-core-synch-l1-2-0.dll (Windows 8+), so it cannot load on the
+  // systems this distribution exists for -- `npm i` dies in its binding.js
+  // with "Cannot find native binding", which is its generic message and not
+  // the npm optional-dependency bug it names (the prebuilds are bundled, so
+  // there is no optional dependency to miss). extract-zip is pure JavaScript
+  // down to yauzl, so it has no such floor.
+  //
+  // Still required lazily, as upstream does: it keeps `isInstalled()` working
+  // when the extractor cannot load at all, so an already-installed dist never
+  // needs it (see electron/electron#52481).
+  const extract = require('extract-zip');
 
   const distPath = process.env.ELECTRON_OVERRIDE_DIST_PATH || path.join(__dirname, 'dist');
 
